 W4 left, which are not a pair. So I need another tile to pair one of them. So I need to draw T5 and then later draw W2 or W4 or something to make a pair. That's two draws.\n\nAlternatively, if I draw W2, then I have a pair W2. Then remaining: W4, T4, T6, and still need a chow. Could draw T5 for chow. So still two draws.\n\nSo it's not a ready hand.\n\nMaybe I should aim for Seven Pairs? I have 8 tiles, could try to form pairs. But I have a triplet W3, which could be broken into a pair and a single. Seven Pairs requires seven distinct pairs. My current hand: W2 (1), W3 (3), W4 (1), W9 (1), T4 (1), T6 (1). That's 8 tiles: one triplet and five singles. Not good for seven pairs.\n\nGiven the situation, I think I need to discard a tile that is less useful and keeps flexibility. W9 is a terminal, and I have no other W9, and it doesn't connect with others. T4 and T6 are in the same suit and could form a chow with T5, but they are separated by one gap. W2, W3, W4 are connected. W3 triplet is valuable as a pung, which gives points (Pung of terminals or honors? W3 is simple, not terminal/honor, so no point). But having a pung is good for All Pungs pattern (6 points) if I can get four pungs. But I only have one pung (F4) and possibly W3 pung, so two pungs. Need two more pungs, unlikely.\n\nConsidering the unshown tiles: W2:2, W4:2, W9:3, T4:3, T6:3. So T4 and T6 have 3 each left, so drawing a pair for them is possible. T5 has 3 left? Unshown T5:3. So drawing T5 for chow is possible.\n\nMaybe discard W9 because it's isolated and a terminal, reducing chance for All Simples etc. But discarding W9 might be safe? Looking at history: W9 has been played by me earlier (Play W9), and others? Not many. Unshown W9:3, so many left. But terminal tiles are dangerous for giving points to others. However, other players have melds: previous player has T7T8T9 chow and T3 kong, so they might be working on terminals? They also played T1, T2, etc. Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, discard T4 or T6? They are middle tiles, might be safer. But they are needed for potential chow.\n\nGiven I have a lot of W tiles, maybe I should aim for a hand with W suit focus. I have W2,W3,W3,W3,W4. That's a strong shape. If I discard T4 and T6, I could aim for a hand mostly in W with honors. But I already have F4 pung, so maybe Half Flush (honor + one suit) worth 6 points. Half Flush: only honor tiles and suited tiles of one suit. My suited tiles are W and T. If I discard all T tiles, I can have only W and honors. I have F4 pung (honor), and W tiles. That could lead to Half Flush if I win with only W and honors. But I have T4 and T6 in hand, so I need to discard them. Also, my meld includes W1W2W3 chow, which is W suit. So if I get rid of T tiles, my hand will be only W and F4. That qualifies for Half Flush (6 points). Plus Prevalent Wind (2), Seat Wind (2), Pung of terminals or honors (1) = 11 points, enough. So that's a good plan.\n\nSo I should discard T4 or T6. Which one? T4 and T6 are both T suit. Discarding either moves toward Half Flush. But which is safer? Looking at discards: previous player played T4, T2, T1, etc. Next player played T2, T7. Across player played T7, T9, T1. T4 has been played by previous player (Play T4) and by me? I played T1, T3, T6 earlier. T6 I played (Play T6). So both have been played. Unshown T4:3, T6:3. So many left. Probably both safe.\n\nBut maybe keep T6 because it pairs with my earlier play? Not important.\n\nAlso, consider that if I discard T4, I still have T6, which is not W, so Half Flush requires no tiles from other suits. So I need to discard both T4 and T6 eventually. This turn I discard one, next turn if I draw a W or honor, I can discard the other. So either is fine.\n\nWhich discard leaves better tile acceptance? If I discard T4, hand: W2, W3, W3, W3, W4, W9, T6. Still have T6, need to discard later. If I discard T6, hand: W2, W3, W3, W3, W4, W9, T4. Similar.\n\nMaybe discard T4 because T4 is closer to T5? But not sure.\n\nAlso, I have W9 which is terminal, not good for Half Flush because Half Flush allows honors and one suit, terminals are allowed if they are in that suit. W9 is W suit, so it's okay for Half Flush. So W9 can stay.\n\nSo plan: aim for Half Flush by discarding T tiles. Also, try to form melds from W tiles. My W tiles: W2, W3x3, W4, W9. I can form pung W3, and then need one more meld and pair. Possible meld: chow W2W3W4 (using one W3). Then pair could be W9 or another W. So I need to draw W9 or W2 or W4 or another W3? Actually, after forming chow W2W3W4 and pung W3, I have left: W9 and the pair tile? Let's simulate: Suppose I keep W2, W3, W3, W3, W4, W9. After discarding T4 and T6 (over two turns), hand is these 6 W tiles. I need one more tile to make 7? Wait, after discarding"}
